    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If a party backs out, the money doesn't just vanish; it gets returned to the person who put it there, minus any fees or legitimate expenses incurred by the other side, all overseen by the escrow agent.\n\nIf the buyer decides to stop, the escrow agent will release the earnest money back to the buyer's account, usually within a few days, unless the contract says the seller can keep it as a penalty for breaking the deal. If the seller decides to walk away, the agent releases the buyer's down payment and the earnest money back to the buyer, and then returns the seller's deposit to the seller.\n\nSometimes, if the deal falls through because of a specific reason like a failed inspection or a financing issue, the contract might have a \"forfeiture\" clause where the seller keeps the earnest money as compensation for the trouble caused. In those cases, the escrow agent simply holds the money for a short period to ensure no one tries to steal it, then transfers it to the seller according to the written agreement.\n\nThe whole process is designed to prevent either side from walking away with the house and the other person's money, ensuring that the funds are only released when the transaction is officially complete or legally",
